---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="Broader Impacts & Prior NSF Support"
  eyebrow="Broader Impacts · Results from Prior NSF Support"
  section="(g), (h)"
  lede="Expanding research access for students from institutions with limited advanced computing resources, while strengthening collaboration across AI, HPC, cybersecurity, and software engineering."
  description="Broader impacts of the ASPIRE REU Site and results from prior NSF support."
>
  <div class="wrap section">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0" id="impacts">Broader impacts</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-4">
      <div class="card">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0">Workforce development</h3>
        <p>
          The Site helps develop the next generation of the STEM and AI-ready workforce through hands-on
          research at the intersection of AI and HPC — aligning with national priorities and NSF's
          FY 2026–2030 strategic priorities in STEM and AI-ready workforce development.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0">Expanded research access</h3>
        <p>
          The program expands research opportunities for students from institutions with limited access to
          undergraduate research and advanced computing resources, and gives participants access to TTU's
          research community and NSF-funded advanced computing resources such as REPACSS.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0">Student dissemination</h3>
        <p>
          Students share their research through presentations and, as projects develop, pursue conference
          presentations and peer-reviewed publications — building on their interests, knowledge, and
          strengths through mentoring and professional development.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0">Research community contributions</h3>
        <p>
          The Site strengthens collaborations across AI, HPC, cybersecurity, and software engineering, and
          contributes research methods, software, and findings to the broader research community.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="prior">Results from prior NSF support</h2>
    <h3>Dr. Chen — IUCRC Center for Cloud and Autonomic Computing (CAC@TTU), Phases I and II</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Award</th><th>Amount</th><th>Period</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Phase I</td><td>CNS-1362134</td><td>$400,000</td><td>4/1/2014 – 3/31/2020</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Phase II</td><td>CNS-1939140</td><td>$500,000</td><td>3/15/2020 – 2/28/2027</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin-top:0">Intellectual merit</h4>
        <p style="font-size:.93rem;color:var(--text-secondary)">
          CAC@TTU focused on research and development innovation in cloud computing best practices,
          algorithm and software design, software stacks, reference implementations, and industry
          applications in real-world settings — with a special focus on data center automation, analytics,
          and control. The effort met industry needs to organize, classify, and develop reference
          implementations, and contributed to advanced distributed computing. Without coordination on
          interface standards, algorithms, and techniques, many potential advantages of these methods would
          be harder to realize amid a chaotic multiplicity of approaches, protocols, and APIs.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin-top:0">Broader impacts</h4>
        <p style="font-size:.93rem;color:var(--text-secondary)">
          The project leveraged contacts with outreach-oriented organizations: the nationally recognized
          TTU STEM Education and Outreach project, and extensions of participant activities into
          high-performance and cloud computing educational dissemination. The Center developed an annual
          activity integrating hands-on learning for high school and REU students, and formed a chapter of
          Women in High Performance Computing during Phase I. CAC@TTU also engaged non-profit organizations
          and carried out over two dozen cloud interoperability plugfests teaching developers about the
          standards and tools developed within the program.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stat-row" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <div class="stat"><div class="num">44</div><div class="lbl">publications listed as results</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="num">8</div><div class="lbl">software deliverables</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="num">2</div><div class="lbl">other deliverables</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="num">24+</div><div class="lbl">cloud interoperability plugfests</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <b>Continuity with this proposal.</b> The prior award established the data-center automation,
      monitoring, and analytics expertise that Projects 1–3 build on, the container and security work that
      Project 5 extends, and the record of integrating REU and high-school students into an active research
      center. <a href={`${base}/projects`}>See the projects →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
